(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-copd-vignettes"]={"title":"Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 79-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease cannot generate a strong inhalation, and his rheumatoid arthritis makes it hard to load and handle his current dry powder inhaler. Which device is most appropriate for him?","choices":["Metered-dose inhaler with a spacer","A different dry powder inhaler","Daily oral corticosteroids","Stopping inhaled therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Arthritis and low peak inspiratory flow limit dry powder inhalers in older adults, so a pressurized metered-dose inhaler with a spacer, a soft mist inhaler or a nebulizer is considered. The spacer reduces the need for a forceful, well-timed breath. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 66-year-old thin man with a long smoking history breathes through pursed lips. He has a barrel chest, distant breath sounds and hyperresonance to percussion. His diffusing capacity is reduced, and he produces little sputum. Which subtype best describes his disease?","choices":["Chronic bronchitis subtype","Asthma","Pulmonary fibrosis","Emphysema subtype"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A thin patient with pursed-lip breathing, barrel chest, distant breath sounds and hyperresonance, little sputum, and a reduced diffusing capacity is the classic emphysema picture (the pink puffer), from alveolar destruction and loss of elastic recoil. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 15, 20"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has an exacerbation with increased, purulent sputum. A sputum culture during her last admission grew Pseudomonas aeruginosa. She is stable for outpatient care. Which oral antibiotic is most appropriate?","choices":["Doxycycline","Amoxicillin-clavulanate","Azithromycin","Levofloxacin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her increased, purulent sputum meets the indication for antibiotics, and a previous Pseudomonas culture creates pseudomonal concern. Levofloxacin is the oral choice for pseudomonal concern, a respiratory fluoroquinolone that also covers atypical, gram-positive and gram-negative organisms. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 59, 62"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman with group E chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is recovering from a hospitalization. She is breathless walking around her home and has never been referred for rehabilitation. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Pulmonary rehabilitation","Thoracic surgery","Behavioral health alone","No referral because of her age"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary rehabilitation is essential for groups B and E and, next to smoking cessation, tends to be the most effective therapy, reducing dyspnea and readmissions. Older adults are less often referred, so she should be referred actively. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 43, 53, 55"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease says she walks slower than friends her age on level ground because of breathlessness, but she does not need to stop. Which modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade fits?","choices":["Grade 0","Grade 1","Grade 2","Grade 3"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Grade 2 is walking slower than people of the same age on the level because of breathlessness, or having to stop for breath at one's own pace on the level. A grade of 2 or more counts as more symptoms. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and paroxysmal atrial fibrillation takes a long-acting beta agonist, a long-acting antimuscarinic and an inhaled corticosteroid; he rarely uses albuterol. His atrial fibrillation episodes and palpitations have become more frequent since his regimen was started. Which component is most likely contributing?","choices":["The long-acting antimuscarinic","The inhaled corticosteroid","His occasional albuterol","The long-acting beta agonist"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Long-acting beta agonists produce sympathetic stimulation that can cause tachycardia and arrhythmias and can make atrial fibrillation worse; their cardiac effects are a listed concern in older adults. With rare albuterol use, the daily beta agonist is the likely driver. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 38, 53"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease comes for a routine visit in October. She has not had any vaccines in the past 5 years. Which vaccines are due now?","choices":["Wait until her next exacerbation","Influenza and pneumococcal vaccines","Influenza vaccine alone","Pneumococcal vaccine alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Every patient should get a yearly influenza shot, proven to reduce hospitalizations and death, and every patient should receive the pneumococcal vaccine at age 65 to reduce community-acquired pneumonia. At 66 and unvaccinated, she needs both. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease uses his albuterol inhaler 10 to 12 times a day and has noticed palpitations and anxiety. Which explanation should guide counseling?","choices":["He should stop albuterol entirely","His symptoms come from the antimuscarinic","Excess albuterol carries cardiac risks","Albuterol use is harmless at any amount"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Every patient should have albuterol, but it should not be used excessively because of toxicity and the risk of cardiac and psychiatric side effects. His heavy use also signals that his maintenance therapy and group need review. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman who has never smoked has progressive exertional dyspnea. Post-bronchodilator spirometry shows a ratio of 0.58, and her chest x-ray shows emphysema mainly at the lung bases. Her father had emphysema in his 40s. Which underlying cause is most likely?","choices":["Tobacco smoke exposure","Household biomass smoke","Asthma with remodeling","Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Emphysema in a young person (45 or under), in a nonsmoker, predominantly basilar on the chest x-ray, and with a family history of emphysema are the suggestive features of alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, which causes panlobular, lower-lobe disease. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 10, 14, 19"},{"q":"An 80-year-old woman with advanced chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, frequent admissions and declining function lives with her daughter, who helps with her care. She has not discussed her wishes for future care. Which step is most appropriate now?","choices":["Integrate palliative care early","Wait for the final days of life","Focus on her returning to work","Add triple therapy and recheck in a year"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. For older adults, palliative care is integrated early, with caregiver involvement and advance directives, and priorities shift to function, quality of life, multimorbidity and goals of care. Her daughter's involvement makes it timely. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 70% predicted, a 6-minute walk distance of 420 meters, a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 2, and a body mass index of 25. Based on her BODE (body mass index, obstruction, dyspnea, exercise) index, what is her estimated 4-year survival?","choices":["18%","57%","67%","80%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. 1-second volume 70% (65 or more) = 0 + walk 420 meters (350 or more) = 0 + dyspnea grade 2 = 1 + body mass index 25 (over 21) = 0, for a total of 1. Scores of 0 to 2 predict about 80% 4-year survival. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman has a post-bronchodilator ratio of 0.66 and a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 83% predicted. Which airflow grade fits?","choices":["Grade 1 (mild)","Grade 2 (moderate)","Grade 3 (severe)","Grade 4 (very severe)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her ratio of 0.66 confirms obstruction, and a post-bronchodilator 1-second volume of 83% predicted meets the grade 1 (mild) threshold of 80% or more. Her airflow is near normal even though the ratio is abnormal. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has an exacerbation. After treatment in the office his breathing is somewhat better, but he has new swelling of both legs, he lives alone, and he has no one to help at home. Which step is most appropriate?","choices":["Outpatient oral treatment","Pulmonary rehabilitation referral","Intensive care admission","Hospital admission"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Admission criteria include new physical signs such as edema, significant comorbidities with an exacerbation, age, and lack of home or outpatient resources. His new edema, advanced age and lack of support at home meet several of them. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with long-standing chronic bronchitis-type disease develops distended neck veins that are most prominent during expiration and swelling of both ankles. His blood gas shows chronic hypoxemia. Which complication do these findings suggest?","choices":["Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","Theophylline toxicity","Cor pulmonale","Pneumothorax"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cor pulmonale, right heart strain or failure, is a secondary complication of this disease, seen with the chronic bronchitis picture of cyanosis and edema. Distended jugular veins, particularly during expiration, and edema fit it. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 17, 21"},{"q":"An 84-year-old woman has mild breathlessness she attributes to deconditioning. Post-bronchodilator spirometry shows a ratio of 0.68 with a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 85% predicted. Which consideration applies before labeling her with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["She needs triple inhaled therapy","The fixed 0.70 ratio may overdiagnose","Any ratio below 0.70 settles it","She needs alpha-1 antitrypsin testing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In adults 65 and older, the fixed 0.70 ratio may overdiagnose, so clinical context is weighed. A borderline ratio with near-normal airflow and mild symptoms in an 84-year-old calls for that judgment. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a mild exacerbation with increased breathlessness. She is alert, her vital signs are stable, she has no new signs, and her husband is at home with her. Which setting of care is most appropriate?","choices":["Outpatient management","Hospital ward admission","Intensive care admission","Noninvasive ventilation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. More than 80% of exacerbations can be managed as outpatients. She meets no admission criterion: no marked symptoms despite treatment, no need for noninvasive ventilation, no new signs or arrhythmia, and support at home. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 56, 57"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with frequent exacerbations asks about taking azithromycin every day to prevent them, as a friend does. Which statement about long-term azithromycin is accurate?","choices":["It is routine first-line therapy","It has no side effects","It replaces her inhalers","It is rare and raises resistance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Some patients are put on long-term azithromycin or erythromycin, but this is rare, and it increases the risk of resistance and side effects. Frequent exacerbations despite optimal therapy are a reason for pulmonology referral. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 54, 59"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with confirmed chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a symptom assessment test score of 8 and a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 1. She was admitted to the hospital once in the past year for an exacerbation. Using the 2020 four-group scheme, which group is she in?","choices":["Group C","Group A","Group B","Group D"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A symptom score of 8 and a dyspnea grade of 1 mean fewer symptoms, and 1 exacerbation leading to hospitalization means high risk. In the 2020 scheme, few symptoms with high risk is group C (in the current scheme she would be E). — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 35, 36, 41"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has 3 days of a runny nose and worse breathlessness after a cold. His sputum is unchanged from baseline in amount and color. He is stable for outpatient treatment. Which treatment is not indicated?","choices":["Checking his oxygen saturation","Antibiotics","Short-acting bronchodilators","A short course of oral corticosteroids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Antibiotics are given when breathlessness comes with increased sputum volume and purulence over baseline. His exacerbation follows a viral cold with unchanged sputum, so bronchodilators and corticosteroids are used without an antibiotic. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 56, 58, 59"},{"q":"A 57-year-old smoker has chronic cough and mild breathlessness. Post-bronchodilator spirometry shows a ratio of forced expiratory volume in 1 second to forced vital capacity of 0.76. How should this result be read?","choices":["It confirms the disease","It shows grade 2 disease","It proves asthma","It does not confirm the disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis requires a post-bronchodilator ratio below 0.70, and his is 0.76. Chronic bronchitis without airflow obstruction is not chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, though smoking cessation remains essential for him. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 6, 26"},{"q":"An 81-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has severe hand arthritis, a tremor and mild cognitive impairment. Testing shows a low peak inspiratory flow. Which inhaler device should be avoided?","choices":["A metered-dose inhaler with a spacer","A dry powder inhaler","A nebulizer","A soft mist inhaler"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In older adults, arthritis, tremor, cognitive impairment and low peak inspiratory flow limit dry powder inhalers, which need a strong, fast breath and dexterity. A pressurized metered-dose inhaler with a spacer, a soft mist inhaler or a nebulizer is considered instead. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 55% predicted, a 6-minute walk distance of 160 meters, a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 1, and a body mass index of 27. Based on his BODE (body mass index, obstruction, dyspnea, exercise) index, what is his estimated 4-year survival?","choices":["18%","57%","67%","80%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. 1-second volume 55% (50 to 64) = 1 + walk 160 meters (150 to 249) = 2 + dyspnea grade 1 = 0 + body mass index 27 (over 21) = 0, for a total of 3. Scores of 3 to 4 predict about 67% 4-year survival. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with confirmed chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a symptom assessment test score of 7 and a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 1. He has had no exacerbations in the past year. Under the current three-group scheme, which group is he in?","choices":["Group E","Group C","Group A","Group B"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A symptom score of 7 (under 10) and a dyspnea grade of 1 (0 to 1) mean fewer symptoms, and no exacerbations means low risk. Low symptoms with low risk is group A, which starts with a single bronchodilator. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 37, 40, 41"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is admitted with an exacerbation. She is alert and hemodynamically stable, but her blood gas shows rising carbon dioxide with respiratory acidosis despite bronchodilators, corticosteroids and antibiotics. Which support is most appropriate?","choices":["Long-term azithromycin","Noninvasive ventilation","High-flow nasal cannula","Discharge with albuterol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Noninvasive ventilation is used for hypercapnic respiratory failure in an exacerbation and reduces intubation rates and mortality. Her alertness and stable circulation make her a candidate, and it is one of the 3 therapies with a survival benefit. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 58, 66"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man going home after an exacerbation asks when he will feel back to normal. Which counseling is most accurate?","choices":["Recovery can take 4 to 6 weeks","He will be back to normal in 2 or 3 days","He will return to baseline without fail","Recovery takes at least 6 months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patient education at discharge includes the recovery timeline: recovery can take 4 to 6 weeks, and he may not return to his previous baseline. Setting that expectation helps him follow through with inhalers, rehabilitation and follow-up. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has had 4 exacerbations this year despite correct technique and adherence to triple inhaled therapy, completed pulmonary rehabilitation and smoking cessation. Which step is most appropriate?","choices":["Repeat spirometry in 1 year","Referral to pulmonology","Daily oral corticosteroids","Stopping the inhaled corticosteroid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Frequent exacerbations despite optimal therapy is a listed reason for pulmonology referral, alongside diagnostic uncertainty, early onset, long-term oxygen, evaluation for noninvasive ventilation, possible surgery, and symptoms out of proportion to testing. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease had 3 moderate exacerbations in the past year. Her blood eosinophil count is 110. She is starting maintenance therapy. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["A single bronchodilator","Daily oral corticosteroids","Dual long-acting bronchodilators","Triple inhaled therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Three moderate exacerbations make her high risk, group E, which starts on a long-acting beta agonist plus a long-acting antimuscarinic. Her eosinophil count of 110 is below 300, so an inhaled corticosteroid, with its pneumonia risk, is not added. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a resting oxygen saturation of 93%, but during a walking test she drops to 85%. She is mobile and reports exertional breathlessness. Which oxygen plan is indicated?","choices":["Oxygen just at night","Oxygen during exertion","Oxygen over 15 hours a day","No oxygen for now"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Severe exertional desaturation, 88% or less with exertion, qualifies for long-term oxygen during exertion. Her resting saturation of 93% is moderate desaturation, without a resting indication, so oxygen is used with activity. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman is being discharged after a hospitalization for an exacerbation. Her blood eosinophil count is elevated. Which action belongs in her discharge bundle?","choices":["Delay rehabilitation for 6 months","Start long-acting bronchodilators promptly","Continue oral steroids indefinitely","Hold inhalers until follow-up"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The discharge bundle begins long-acting bronchodilators promptly, considers an inhaled corticosteroid if eosinophils are elevated (as hers are), checks inhaler technique, considers pulmonary rehabilitation, arranges follow-up and explains the recovery timeline. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 61, 55"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease was started on daily oral prednisone by another clinic 6 months ago as maintenance therapy. He has had no recent exacerbation. Which part of his regimen should be stopped or avoided?","choices":["A long-acting antimuscarinic","A yearly influenza vaccine","Maintenance oral corticosteroids","An albuterol rescue inhaler"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Oral corticosteroids are for acute exacerbations only in this disease, which is why they are missing from the list of common chronic therapies. Maintenance is built from albuterol as needed and long-acting inhaled bronchodilators, with an inhaled corticosteroid only when indicated. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 49"}]};
